inality constraint k  is also called a budget  this problem is solved decades ago  specifically there is a greedy algorithm  that achieves 1 minus 1 over e  approximation of the optimal value  basically the gradient algorithm starts  from an empty solution x  and it runs in k iterations in each  iteration selects the element  that has the largest marginal value to  the current solution and add  the element to the current solution  it turns out  greedy algorithm is also optimal because  there is a matching hardness result that  says it is impossible to beat one method  over e using efficient algorithm  more recent research focuses on  designing streaming parallel robust  or faster algorithms that get 1 minus 1  over e of the optimal value  so what about the missing one over e the  point is 1 minus 1 over e is really just  a worst case guarantee  in practice people observe greedy does  much better  how much better the answer probably  depends a lot on application or instance  there's a recent paper that shows greedy  achieves 80 to 99 approximation for many  realistic instances  a nice open problem in this area is  white really achieves better performance  in practice what makes practical  instances  easier and is there an even better  algorithm than greedy in practice  there are many previous work that try to  answer these questions by modeling  beyond worst case modular functions  for example magnetic curvature measures  how close a module function is to an  additive function  coverage and undirected graphs these are  special cases of some modular functions  stability assumes if you perturb this  module function by a little the optimal  solution doesn't change much  random perturbation is basically adding  some noise to some modular functions  finally we have a more recent notion  called sharpness  however these proposals they suffer from  two kinds of issues  either they're just too strong  assumption and intractable to verify in  practice  or there simply can't beat one minus one  of three approximation  so in summary we have a lot of great  ideas  but we really don't understand what is a  nice modular function  therefore we are faced with beyond the  worst case diagram  on the one hand we want performance  we want to account for the missing one  over e or at least a fraction of it  on the other hand we want robustness  we want our model to apply widely to  practical instances  and we want the modeling assumption to  be easy to verify in practice  our approach to resolves dilemma is  starting from an overlooked object  recall that the modular maximization  problem has two objects  as modular function f and the budget  constraint k  instead of modeling beyond worst case  modular functions we can also model  beyond the worst case behavior of the  much simpler object the budget  this approach is motivated by both  practice and theory  and we will show we can get both  performance and the robustness  in practice many applications of some  manual maximization have many users with  the same objective function for  different budgets  for example in influence maximization  multiple advertisers try to maximize  their influence  in the same social network  therefore optimize essentially the same  as the modular function  however their budgets can easily differ  by an order of magnitude because of  different business sites  in another example of feature selection  machine learning engineers try to select  features for the same machine learning  model in the same data set  therefore maximize essentially the same  approximately submodular function  however the number of features they  would like to select  could easily range from 100 to 1 million  because of different computational  resources they have  finally  in practice verifying beyond worst case  assumptions for budgets  is much more tractable than for  functions  because we just need to calculate the  empirical distribution of the budgets  in theory all the existing hard  instances break under significant  observation of budget  specifically given a hard instance  namely as a modular function f and the  budget k  consider the following new instance  where this module function is still f  but the budget is changed to either 10  times k or k divided by 10.  for the new instance even outputting a  random solution would achieve 95  approximation  therefore we revisit some modular  maximization by considering average case  behavior of the budget  specifically we are interested in the  following questions  first  what's the optimal algorithm given  distribution of budgets  is grid is still optimal  second  does average case behavior of the budget  allows optimal algorithm to achieve  better than 1 minus 1 over e  approximation  in expectation  if not  can we prove a stronger hardness result  that is robust to any budget  distribution  to formally answer these questions we  introduce a framework  called budget smooth analysis  the name is inspired by the celebrated  smooth analysis by experiment in the  turn  admittedly  the analogy is not perfect  because we need a significant provision  of the budget  now let me introduce our model budget  smooth analysis  first we fix the budget distribution  for example the budget distribution of  2020 democrats candidates campaign  then the university picks a single worst  case modular function f  arguably all the candidates are  optimizing roughly the same function but  have very different budgets  for example they could use their budgets  to maximize the influence over the same  social network  which i have explained earlier  then we sample a random budget from the  distribution  finally we run the algorithm on the  instance given by f and k  our analysis shows in this model  grid guarantees that average candidates  campaign was 67  optimal  let's do this one more time but a bit  more formally  first we fix the budget distribution d  and then adversary fix a single worst  case modular function f  after that 